 ## Overview
-MS-NeRF (Multi-Space Neural Radiance Field) is a scene representation method that extends standard MLP-based NeRF architectures to handle unbounded virtual scenes containing reflective and refractive surfaces, such as mirrors [S1, S2]. It solves the problem of rendering distortion and blurring caused by reflection or refraction, where standard NeRF fails to maintain multi-view consistency due to the geometric complexity of virtual images [S2]. Unlike sibling methods like Ref-NeRF, which require manually labeled masks for reflective surfaces and are limited to forward-facing scenes, MS-NeRF automatically handles 360-degree scenes without extra supervision [S1]. It sets itself apart by decomposing the radiance field into $K$ parallel sub-spaces, each representing a characteristic field, and integrating them via a learned gating mechanism [S2].
+MS-NeRF (Multi-Space Neural Radiance Field) is a scene representation method that extends standard MLP-based NeRF architectures to handle unbounded virtual scenes containing reflective and refractive surfaces [S1, S2]. It solves the problem of rendering distortion and blurring caused by reflection or refraction, where standard NeRF fails to maintain multi-view consistency due to the geometric complexity of virtual images [S2]. Unlike sibling methods like Ref-NeRF, which require manually labeled masks for reflective surfaces and are limited to forward-facing scenes, MS-NeRF automatically handles 360-degree scenes without extra supervision [S1]. It sets itself apart by decomposing the radiance field into $K$ parallel sub-spaces, each representing a characteristic field, and integrating them via a learned gating mechanism [S2].
 
